    <h2 id="how">How Does Saffron Work?</h2>
    <p>Saffron's main active compounds are <strong>crocin</strong>, <strong>crocetin</strong>, <strong>safranal</strong> and <strong>picrocrocin</strong>. Lab and animal research suggests they act in several ways:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Neurotransmitters:</strong> crocin and safranal appear to influence serotonin, dopamine and norepinephrine signalling, which could explain the mood effects.</li>
      <li><strong>Antioxidant and anti-inflammatory activity:</strong> crocin is a potent antioxidant, which may protect retinal cells and neurons.</li>
      <li><strong>Stress hormones:</strong> some trials found changes in cortisol, which may matter for stress and sleep.</li>
      <li><strong>Retinal blood flow:</strong> crocetin may help regulate how retinal cells respond to light and oxidative stress.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The human trials are what count, though, so below each benefit is rated by the quality of the clinical evidence.</p>

