    <h2 id="mood">1. Saffron for Mood and Mild Depression</h2>
    <p>This is saffron's best-researched use. Meta-analyses pooling randomized controlled trials have found that saffron improves depressive symptoms compared with placebo. Several head-to-head trials found 30 mg/day roughly as effective as fluoxetine or imipramine for <em>mild to moderate</em> depression over 6–8 weeks (Hausenblas 2013; Toth 2019; Marx 2019). In healthy adults with low mood, 28 mg/day of affron® improved mood scores in 4 weeks (Kell 2017).</p>
    <p class="note"><strong>Important:</strong> Most of these trials were small and short, and many were run in Iran by a small number of research groups. Saffron is not a replacement for treatment of moderate or severe depression. If you are struggling, please speak to a doctor.</p>
    <p><a href="/saffron-for-depression-and-mood/">Full guide: Saffron for depression and mood &rarr;</a></p>

    <h2 id="anxiety">2. Saffron for Stress and Anxiety</h2>
    <p>The 2019 meta-analysis by Marx and colleagues found saffron also reduced anxiety symptoms. A trial in adolescents with mild-to-moderate anxiety or low mood found 28 mg/day of affron® improved symptoms as reported by the teenagers themselves (Lopresti 2018). The evidence is thinner than for mood, but the signal is consistent.</p>

    <h2 id="sleep">3. Saffron for Sleep Quality</h2>
    <p>In a 28-day trial of adults with self-reported poor sleep, affron® taken at 14 mg twice daily improved sleep quality scores and mood on waking compared with placebo (Lopresti 2020). Saffron isn't a sedative, so don't expect it to knock you out like a sleeping pill. It may help sleep feel more restorative, especially when stress is part of the problem.</p>
    <p><a href="/saffron-for-sleep/">Full guide: Saffron for sleep &rarr;</a></p>

    <h2 id="eyes">4. Saffron for Eye Health (Macular Degeneration)</h2>
    <p>Small Italian trials gave 20 mg/day of saffron to people with early age-related macular degeneration (AMD) and found improved retinal flicker sensitivity, a measure of how well the retina responds to light (Falsini 2010; Piccardi 2012). An Australian crossover trial found a modest benefit in mild/moderate AMD, including in people already taking AREDS-type supplements (Broadhead 2019).</p>
    <p><a href="/saffron-for-eye-health/">Full guide: Saffron for eye health &rarr;</a></p>

    <h2 id="pms">5. Saffron for PMS</h2>
    <p>A double-blind trial in women with PMS found 15 mg of saffron twice daily over two menstrual cycles reduced total PMS symptoms and depressive symptoms compared with placebo (Agha-Hosseini 2008).</p>
    <p><a href="/saffron-for-pms/">Full guide: Saffron for PMS &rarr;</a></p>

    <h2 id="libido">6. Saffron for Libido and Sexual Function</h2>
    <p>Two trials looked at sexual side effects from SSRI antidepressants. In men taking fluoxetine, 15 mg of saffron twice daily improved erectile function scores (Modabbernia 2012). In women, the same dose improved arousal, lubrication and pain scores (Kashani 2013). These are small studies, and evidence for libido in people not on SSRIs is limited.</p>

    <h2 id="appetite">7. Saffron for Appetite and Snacking</h2>
    <p>Mildly overweight women who took Satiereal® (176.5 mg/day) for 8 weeks snacked less often and lost slightly more weight than the placebo group (Gout 2010). The difference was modest. Saffron can help with between-meal cravings, but it is not a fat burner.</p>
    <p><a href="/saffron-for-weight-loss/">Full guide: Saffron for weight loss &rarr;</a></p>

    <h2 id="memory">Memory and Cognition (Early Research)</h2>
    <p>A 16-week trial in people with mild-to-moderate Alzheimer's disease found saffron 30 mg/day improved cognitive scores compared with placebo (Akhondzadeh 2010). It is a single small study. Saffron should not be used in place of prescribed dementia care, and evidence for memory in healthy people is very limited.</p>

